Thus Ax's result 5.0 supports the following conjecture, suggested by the
philosophy of the level of motives and relating the classical Hodge and
Tate conjectures~[5].

\subsubsection*{Conjecture 5.2.}
\underline{Let} $f:X\to S$ \underline{be a proper smooth morphism}, \underline{with} $S$ \underline{integral}, \underline{of finite
type over} $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb Z)$, \underline{and dominant over}
$\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb Z)$; \underline{let} $i,j$ \underline{be integers}.  \underline{Suppose that the
Hodge coniveau of} $H^i$ \underline{of the generic fiber of} $f$ \underline{is} at least $j$.  \underline{Then,
for every point} $s\in S$, \underline{the coniveau} (XX, 2.0.6) \underline{of}
$H^i(X_s,\mathbb Q_\ell)$ \underline{is} at least $j$.  \underline{Hence} (XX, 2.2), \underline{if} $s$ \underline{is a
closed point of} $S$, \underline{the eigenvalues of Frobenius acting on}
$H^i(X_s,\mathbb Q_\ell)$ \underline{are algebraic integers divisible by} $q^j$, \underline{where}
$q=\operatorname{card}(k(s))$.

